\begin{abstract}
Practitioners select among hybrid linear recurrent architectures using aggregate benchmarks, but these scores can mask which specific examples each architecture gets right. Across three controlled designs, adding attention affects substantially different examples per architecture (Jaccard $\approx$ 0.10, approximately twice the independence baseline); validation across five architectures and ten pairwise comparisons confirms this on all four benchmarks (all $p < .01$). Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid} (three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales), we decompose per-example effects and find that this predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} depends on task type ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) more than attention dose. Aggregate trends do not reach statistical significance ($n = 5$; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$), yet per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention and remains flat across all four benchmarks. Majority vote fails to exploit this complementarity, confirming that configurations interchangeable by aggregate metrics carry hidden per-example consequences. Confidence-informed routing extracts 36\% of oracle complementarity on LAMBADA ($+5.2$\,pp vs.\ majority vote), suggesting these differences are partially actionable.
\end{abstract}

